      {
        "position": 6,
        "title": "Decent Pair, but not worth the price!",
        "text": "Honestly, this one was a bit of a letdown for me. I usually really like stuff from Tweezerman, their regular tweezers are usually super precise and feel high quality, but this one just doesn’t hit the same.First thing I noticed was the alignment. The two jaws aren’t as well lined up as you’d expect, especially compared to their normal tweezers. It makes grabbing smaller stuff more annoying than it should be, which kind of defeats the purpose.Overall, it just feels like it’s on the lower end of quality for their brand. It’s not terrible, but it definitely doesn’t have that “premium Tweezerman” feel you’d expect.Another weird issue is the design, because of the angle on one side, it doesn’t really sit right in the included pouch. It’s kind of awkward getting it in and out, which makes the case feel more like an afterthought than something actually useful.For the price, I just don’t think it’s worth it. There are better options out there, and honestly even other Tweezerman products are way better than this.I wouldn’t recommend these, especially for what they cost.",
        "rating": 3,
        "date": "April 10, 2026",
        "author": "TankPrime99",
        "author_link": "https://www.amazon.com/gp/profile/amzn1.account.AHODJ5BBAJVQPOAB3YALDVDQNPOQ?ref=cm_cr_dp_d_bdcrb_top",
        "author_image": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/S/amazon-avatars-global/default._SX48_.png",
        "helpful_votes": "2 people found this helpful"
      },
      {
        "position": 7,
        "title": "Tweezerman makes a good product",
        "text": "I live in tick country and have a dog that likes to lay and play in the grass. Ticks are an unpleasant fact of life. We've had Lyme disease, anaplasmosis and erlichia at various times. We need a good method for removing ticks when we find them on the dog.This is a very nice dual purpose removal tool. I haven't had occasion to use the \"scooper\" side yet but I know from experience with other scooper type tick tools that it will get the job done. What I like about the tweezer side is that the point ends aren't too sharp and pointy so when you're pressing them into the dog they aren't likely to scratch or puncture skin as some tweezers might. They're easier to use for that reason.These are well made and feel like quality that will last. I found them to be sized well for my hands and comfortable to hold if that makes sense. I love that they come with their own pouch and that the pouch has a hook. Great for carrying it with me when we're out in the woods walking.The only reason I debated giving it 4 instead of 5 stars is because I think they are a bit pricey for what you're getting. However there is something to be said for the fact that they are tweezerman brand. I know they make a very reliable product. And I'm not sure how they do it but they manage to make even the most mundane products attractive. You know it's a tweezerman if it looks good, lol.BOTTOM LINE: another great tweezerman product that is built to last. I do think it's a bit overpriced but I don't fault the quality.",
        "rating": 5,
        "date": "May 3, 2026",
        "author": "a long way from home",
        "author_link": "https://www.amazon.com/gp/profile/amzn1.account.AF5VNJUGCPRJ7AKOZYXPX56BO3LQ?ref=cm_cr_dp_d_bdcrb_top",
        "author_image": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/S/amazon-avatars-global/e768d66d-a599-4a96-80ab-7aad70ec4653._CR0%2C0%2C851%2C851_SX460_SX48_.jpg",
        "helpful_votes": "3 people found this helpful"
      },
      {
        "position": 8,
        "title": "These are exactly what you need",
        "text": "Lyme disease is no laughing matter. I go everywhere with my dogs. They hunt, they do tracking and field work. We hike, we camp. As a result, ticks are a continuing concern. I've pulled as many as 30 ticks off a dog in a single session before, and I want to get them off cleanly and as rapidly as possible - the less time the ticks are on your dog, the lower the chance of them transmitting lyme disease is. Obviously, prevention is your best option - avoiding areas with ticks, using treatments (medicines/collars) to prevent ticks from latching on, etc. These are basically your final line of that defense.Anyway, I've had another set of Tweezerman tick removal tweezers in a custom \"dog first aid\" kit I put together and keep in my truck. It has worked well on multiple occasions. This set is basically the same with the tool ends, but even nicer, due to finish and the handy case. You'll definitely want to keep these in the case, as the tips are pretty pokey otherwise, and if you're trying to keep these somewhere handy in your camping/exploring/first aid gear, you don't want them tearing up the rest of yourself, or cause an injury while you grab them in a hurry, skeeved out by the ticks you're about to pull.Anyway, so I really appreciate this set. While these are a little on the expensive side for \"tweezers\", understand that these are a precision tool that is finely crafted to do one specific job that they do very well. And if they at all help you correctly pull a tick without leaving anything behind, they quite likely will help you avoid infected wounds and help reduce the potential for lyme disease. These are definitely cheaper than a vet bill and FAR cheaper than a lifetime of treatments for Lyme disease.",
